---
import PolicyLayout from "../layouts/PolicyLayout.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../lib/site-path";
---

<PolicyLayout title="Privacy disclosure" description="How Chaft's desktop canary, peer synchronization, website, downloads, and support reports handle information.">
  <p>
    This disclosure covers the current Chaft desktop canary and this website,
    maintained by <a href="https://github.com/Jurshsmith">Jurshsmith</a>.
    Chaft is experimental software for non-sensitive evaluation. It has not
    received an independent security audit.
  </p>

  <h2>Information stored on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Chaft stores device identities and keys, workspace history, messages,
    attachments, profiles, settings, and a search index on participating devices.
    Content is encrypted before replication, but local databases and search
    indexes are not a fully encrypted vault. Protect your device and backups.
    Exports and recovery files you create are additional copies under your control.
  </p>

  <h2>Peer synchronization</h2>
  <p>
    Joining a workspace and configuring peers or replicas enables network
    transfers to those participants. Synchronization can continue while Chaft
    is running without a separate prompt for each message. Encrypted content,
    signed events, and synchronization metadata are transferred to support the
    workspace. Participants and network operators can observe some identifiers,
    membership, timing, endpoint addresses, traffic sizes, and connection metadata.
    Encryption does not make your network activity anonymous.
  </p>
  <p>
    Authorized recipients can read shared content and retain copies. Removing a
    member, deleting local data, or stopping synchronization cannot erase copies
    or keys already held by another device. Replica operators control their own
    storage and retention.
  </p>

  <h2>Optional network services</h2>
  <p>
    Public Iroh relay and discovery are disabled by default. Explicitly enabling
    them can connect to third-party infrastructure and expose endpoint or
    connection metadata to its operators. The public defaults use number 0's
    infrastructure; review its <a href="https://www.iroh.computer/legal">Iroh privacy terms</a>
    and <a href="https://docs.iroh.computer/deployment/security-privacy">network privacy guidance</a>.
    Review any other configured provider's privacy terms before enabling it. See the
    <a href={sitePath("/docs/concepts/networking-and-replication/")}>networking guide</a>
    for the current configuration and limitations. Disable these options to
    avoid those services; direct peer connections still disclose network metadata.
  </p>

  <h2>Diagnostics and reports</h2>
  <p>
    The current desktop app has no automatic usage-analytics or crash-report
    upload feature. Local diagnostic output, screenshots, and exported files
    may contain sensitive information. If you send a report, share only what
    is needed and remove messages, keys, passphrases, invitations, and private
    endpoints. Public GitHub issues and their attachments are publicly visible.
    Use the <a href={sitePath("/security/")}>private reporting process</a> for
    suspected vulnerabilities, and agree on a private channel before sharing
    personal information in a privacy request.
  </p>

  <h2>Website and downloads</h2>
  <p>
    This static website does not add advertising trackers, analytics scripts,
    or account-registration forms. Fonts are served with the site. Browser
    platform detection selects a suggested download locally.
    <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare</a> hosts the
    website and processes request information such as IP addresses and HTTP
    metadata to deliver and protect it. Downloads, source hosting, and issue
    reports use <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub</a>,
    which processes information under its own privacy statement. Following
    external links sends a request to the destination; its policies apply.
  </p>

  <h2>Your controls and retention</h2>
  <p>
    Stop Chaft and any replica processes to stop their network activity. Remove
    configured peer connections or disable optional services before reconnecting.
    To remove a portable Windows installation, quit Chaft and delete its extracted
    application folder. This does not remove profile data. Local history, keys,
    settings, logs, exports, and backups remain until you remove the relevant
    application data and copies. Confirm the active data directory before
    deleting it; deleting keys or the only copy of history can prevent recovery.
    There is no central Chaft account whose deletion erases all workspace copies.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hosting providers and workspace participants have their own retention rules;
    this disclosure does not promise deletion from their systems. Changes to
    Chaft's data handling will be reflected here. Read the
    <a href={sitePath("/security/")}>security boundaries</a> before testing a canary.
  </p>
</PolicyLayout>
